FINAL ANSWER:
**Bus factor of `lib/`: 2.** Take Troy Goode plus one more contributor, and over 50% of commit history has no author left. Practically it is closer to 1, because nobody has committed in the last 90 days.

`lib/` has one file, `lib/index.js`. The numbers below come from `git log` on `lib/`, excluding merge commits.

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Commits touching `lib/` | 66 |
| Distinct authors | 25 |
| Top author (Troy Goode) | 33 commits, 50% |
| Next authors | Douglas C. Wilson 4, Alejandro Caravaca Puchades 4, Simon Mansfield 3, jordandenison 2 |
| Authors with a single commit | 20 |
| Commits in the last 90 days | 0 (latest commit on HEAD is 2026-04-12) |

**Why 2 and not 1:** Troy has exactly 50%, which is not more than 50%. Troy plus Douglas Wilson is 37 of 66 commits (56%), so that pair crosses the threshold.

**Why it is weaker than the number suggests:**
- **Nobody is active.** No author committed in the last 90 days, so current maintenance knowledge is unconfirmed for all 25.
- **The tail is shallow.** 20 authors made one commit each, which means a small patch, not deep ownership. Only about 5 people have 2 or more commits.
- **Some commits carry no knowledge.** The latest commit (`style: reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes`, author "Fiona Formatter") is a mechanical reformat. Commit counts overstate understanding.
- **Single file.** `lib/index.js` is the whole directory, so the directory-level and file-level factors are the same.
- **Commit counts only.** I did not run blame to see how many surviving lines each person wrote. That would show who understands the current code, and it may differ from this ranking.

**Recommendations:**
1. Have a current maintainer read through `lib/index.js` and write down the non-obvious parts, since nobody has committed recently.
2. Run `git blame` on the file to confirm which authors still have live lines.
3. Add review and test coverage around the handlers so a new contributor can change them safely.

